WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:05.950
To celebrate the DVD &amp; Blu-ray release of
Interstellar, let’s take a look at 15 things

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:10.549
you probably didn’t know about Christopher
Nolan’s epic sci-fi movie.

00:00:10.549 --> 00:00:16.070
For Interstellar, Christopher Nolan returned
to the same glacier where he’d filmed Batman

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:17.070
Begins.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:21.810
Since the director’s last visit, a volcanic
eruption had left the glacier scored by grey

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:26.500
ash, but this grittier look gave the location
the feel of a hostile environment, which was

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.610
what he wanted for the film’s Ice Planet.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:34.770
Both Chile and England’s Morecambe Bay were
originally considered as filming locations

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:36.210
for the movie’s Water Planet.

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:40.730
But the Interstellar team found what they
were looking for in the shallow yet seemingly

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:44.970
endless Brunasandur lagoon in Iceland.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:49.990
The film’s costume design team had just
12 weeks to design and build Interstellar’s

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:51.230
astronaut suits.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:56.100
Because of the amount of time the cast spent
wearing the suits, the oxygen units in them

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.719
had to work so the actors could breathe when
they had their helmets on.

00:00:59.719 --> 00:01:04.360
The suits also contained a system of cold
water tubes to stop the actors from getting

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:05.449
too hot.

00:01:05.449 --> 00:01:09.630
And backpacks with integrated air cooling
systems were also made to keep the actors

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:12.030
from overheating and their glass visors fog-free.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:17.820
All in all, the costume weighed between 30
and 35 pounds.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:22.360
The astronauts’ helmets were created using
a 3D printer and were custom fitted for the

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:24.200
actors and their stunt doubles.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:29.440
A sound system was installed inside the helmets
so the cast and director could communicate.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:35.710
Nolan wanted the movie’s mechanical astronauts,
CASE and TARS, to be minimalist in design.

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:40.010
He and production designer Nathan Crowley
tried out possible designs by gluing together

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:44.520
popsicle sticks, and later they tested out
different combinations of earth magnets.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:49.979
They finally decided on a blocky, 5-foot-tall
plank system that unfolds magnetically.

00:01:49.979 --> 00:01:54.619
The movie’s special effects co-ordinator
Scott Fisher and his team built 8 robots for

00:01:54.619 --> 00:01:55.619
the film.

00:01:55.619 --> 00:02:01.180
Fisher estimated that 80% of the robot footage
in Interstellar is practical effects rather

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:06.299
than CGI, which was mainly reserved for especially
complex robotic movement.

00:02:06.299 --> 00:02:12.860
On set, actor Bill Irwin not only performed
the voices of both CASE and TARS, interacting

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:18.410
with the rest of the cast, but he also operated
the robots via a mechanical puppeteering rig.

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:23.570
However, for some scenes that required more
movement, the 200-pound rig was operated by

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:25.569
stuntman Mark Fichera.

00:02:25.569 --> 00:02:30.540
According to Irwin, TARS personality is a
mix of gym teacher and ex-Marine.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:36.400
In the final film, we only hear Irwin as TARS,
while actor Josh Stewart voices CASE.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:43.230
When we see Cooper speeding through corn fields
in his truck, chasing a drone, Matthew McConaughey

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:48.540
is really in the driver’s seat, but it was
actually a stunt man in a special rig on top

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:52.129
of the truck who was operating the vehicle’s
controls.

00:02:52.129 --> 00:02:58.270
For Interstellar’s scenes of life during
dust storms, Nolan was inspired by filmmaker

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:04.730
Ken Burns’s documentary about the effect
on 1930s Depression-era America of the period

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:07.970
of environmental disaster known as the Dust
Bowl.

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:14.090
Nolan even incorporated interviews with real
people from Burns’s documentary into Interstellar.

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:20.060
To create Interstellar’s dust storms, huge
fans were used to fill the air with C-90,

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:25.300
a non-toxic biodegradable material made from
ground-up cardboard.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:31.379
Although Cooper’s home is located in America’s
heartland, filming actually took place just

00:03:31.379 --> 00:03:34.700
south of Calgary, in the Canadian province
of Alberta.

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:39.000
Christopher Nolan didn’t want to shoot a
separate farmhouse, corn field and mountain,

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:40.510
and place them together digitally.

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:45.599
So, after local rancher Rick Sears found a
piece of land that worked for the movie’s

00:03:45.599 --> 00:03:51.050
requirements, he helped the Interstellar team
build a road to the location and plant 500

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:54.030
acres of corn about six months before filming
began.

00:03:54.030 --> 00:04:00.390
For the design of Cooper’s home, Nolan was
inspired by the paintings of artist .

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:06.250
He wanted the house to look contemporary but
not futuristic, and for its architecture to

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:07.680
have a timeless feel.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:12.470
The house was built from scratch by the movie’s
art department in less than 10 weeks and was

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:15.430
pretty much like a real home, but without
plumbing.

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:22.049
Former US astronaut Marsha Ivins shared her
experience of space and life as an astronaut

00:04:22.049 --> 00:04:25.629
with the movie’s cast and crew during a
visit to the Interstellar set.

00:04:25.629 --> 00:04:30.389
Ivins also sent Anne Hathaway video footage
she’d recorded during her space missions

00:04:30.389 --> 00:04:35.300
and gave the cast notes about how to move
in Zero Gravity.

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:41.050
Research by theoretical physicist Kip Thorne
forms the scientific backbone of Interstellar.

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:45.690
Thorne spent time with the Interstellar cast
to help them understand the science behind

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:46.690
the film.

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:51.680
And, to ensure Interstellar’s universe was
as authentic as possible, Thorne also worked

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:55.479
closely with the movie’s visual effects
supervisor Paul Franklin.

00:04:55.479 --> 00:05:00.379
Thorne produced mathematical equations on
the effects of gravity on light around a blackhole

00:05:00.379 --> 00:05:01.939
and through a wormhole.

00:05:01.939 --> 00:05:06.710
Then Franklin and his visual effects team
used those equations to write new software

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:10.759
that created the film’s blackhole and wormhole.

00:05:10.759 --> 00:05:14.949
Christopher Nolan asked Hans Zimmer to start
writing the Interstellar score before the

00:05:14.949 --> 00:05:19.539
script was even finished, and, initially,
he didn’t even tell the composer what genre

00:05:19.539 --> 00:05:20.619
the movie was going to be either.

00:05:20.619 --> 00:05:26.300
Instead of a script, Nolan gave Zimmer a one-page
scene about a father and his relationship

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:27.300
with his son.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:32.080
Zimmer drew on his own personal experiences
as a father to write the first piece of music,

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:36.099
which became the basis for the whole score.

00:05:36.099 --> 00:05:41.789
The large pipe organ at London’s 12th-century
Temple Church features heavily in the film’s

00:05:41.789 --> 00:05:45.650
score and was played by Roger Sayer, Director
of Music at the Church.

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:51.119
The organ was built by Harrison &amp; Harrison
in the 1920s for a ballroom in Glen Tanar

00:05:51.119 --> 00:05:56.610
Castle in Scotland and was relocated to Temple
Church in the 1950s.

00:05:56.610 --> 00:06:00.960
Well there you have it, 15 things you probably
didn't know about Interstellar!

